    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter tonight\u2019s slate with a lineup anchored by the high-upside pairing of **Yordan Alvarez** (82.97) and **Trea Turner** (78.31). **Yordan Alvarez** is our clear offensive engine, benefiting from an elite 0.473 xwOBA projection against Phillips, while **Trea Turner** leverages his 100% stolen base rate against a slow-working starter to maximize our scarcest category. Both **Kyle Schwarber** (72.97) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (64.92) capitalize on favorable dynamics; **Pete Crow-Armstrong** specifically benefits from an aggressive stolen base environment despite a neutral platoon, while **Kyle Schwarber** remains a high-floor start thanks to his premium #2 order placement and park factors.\n\nMiddle-tier production is fortified by a strong platoon edge for **Casey Schmitt** (63.92) and **Lars Nootbaar** (64.23), both of whom see significant boosts from positive SP skill ratings. **Dillon Dingler** (63.90) is a notable start today; while he is penalized slightly by a catcher-theft-prevention-focused opponent, his 14% dynamic platoon boost and #4 order placement make him a vital asset. Conversely, **Kazuma Okamoto** (43.23) represents our most marginal start, as the tough matchup against Rasmussen forces a regression from his base projection. The decision to bench **Caleb Durbin** was ultimately made moot by a confirmed rainout; however, even prior to the postponement, his optimization was trailing the core starters due to the volatility of the matchup. We are closely monitoring **Gary Sanchez** (52.95), who remains a strictly situational play, and we will continue to hold **Christian Yelich**, **Hyeseong Kim**, and **Colt Keith** on the bench while we monitor their status. Our depth, including **Nelson Velazquez**, **Josh Smith**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez**, remains ready should any late-inning tactical shifts be required.",
